Thank you for this insightful comment regarding the evolution of the SAM family (SAM 1 vs. SAM 2 vs. SAM 3).
We would like to clarify the structural motivations and provide detailed empirical/architectural justifications for our design choices:
1. Architectural Trade-offs: 
The native SAM 1 employs a heavy, non-hierarchical Plain ViT backbone (e.g., ViT-H) with global positional embeddings. Because it maintains a high-density, non-strided spatial feature representation throughout the transformer blocks, SAM 1 preserves fine-grained boundary cues and contour geometry with high fidelity on static 2D images.
SAM 2 replaces the plain ViT with a hierarchical vision backbone (Hiera) and introduces multi-scale windowed attention to achieve real-time video memory tracking. While Hiera provides notable runtime speedups, its successive downsampling/pooling stages can compromise localized boundary precision. Recent benchmark studies (e.g., in medical image segmentation with thin/tubular structures such as retinal vessels and micro-cells) demonstrate that SAM 1 (ViT-H) frequently exhibits superior topological continuity and less boundary leakage compared to SAM 2 under single- or few-point prompts.
SAM 3 is primarily engineered for open-vocabulary and multi-modal text/exemplar reasoning rather than isolated, point-prompt-conditioned geometric mask delineation.
2. Empirical Verification on Point-Prompted Geometric Decoding 
